{{!
	ContentHeader.mustache -- the page title at hero scale, the site tagline, core's
	indicators, and the page-tab cluster.

	Reached from skin.mustache as a partial named ContentHeader. It renders on every one of
	the twenty-one captured surfaces, so anything volatile in here would break byte-identity
	(R9) across the whole capture matrix at once, not just on one page. There is nothing
	generated, timed or randomised below, and the four tab groups are emitted in a fixed
	order that does not depend on the data.

	TEMPLATE DATA CONSUMED

	Every key is core's; this partial reads nothing Blitzy-specific and nothing from
	BlitzyViewModel.

	  html-title-heading   Skin::getTemplateData(). The complete h1 element, built by core
	                       with id="firstHeading", class="firstHeading mw-first-heading",
	                       the user-language lang and dir attributes, and
	                       style="display: none" when the heading has been deliberately
	                       blanked. Interpolated raw -- the one lawful raw interpolation of
	                       markup in this file. See NOT EMITTED HERE below for why it is
	                       never rebuilt from html-title.
	  msg-tagline          Core message `tagline`, whose English value is "From " followed by
	                       the site name. SkinMustache::getTemplateData() folds it in through
	                       Message::text(), so it arrives as plain text with magic words
	                       already expanded and must be interpolated escaped.
	  msg-namespaces       Core message `namespaces` ("Namespaces"). Accessible name of the
	                       associated-pages group -- see ACCESSIBLE NAMES.
	  msg-views            Core message `views` ("Views").
	  msg-actions          Core message `actions` ("Actions").
	  msg-variants         Core message `variants` ("Variants").
	  array-indicators     Skin::getIndicatorsData(). Each entry carries exactly three keys:
	                       id (already escaped by core as mw-indicator- plus the indicator
	                       name), class (always the single class mw-indicator) and html.
	  data-portlets        SkinTemplate::getPortletsTemplateData(). Four members are read, in
	                       this order: data-associated-pages, data-views, data-actions,
	                       data-variants. Per portlet this partial uses id, class,
	                       html-tooltip, is-empty, html-before-portal, html-after-portal and
	                       array-items; per item it uses html-item.

	The five message keys above exist only because skin.json lists them in the `messages`
	array of ValidSkinNames.blitzy -- that array is what makes SkinMustache produce the
	msg- prefixed keys. All five are core keys, so this file introduces no new blitzy-
	message and adds nothing for the banana checker to document (Gate 2).

	FIDELITY CONTRACT -- the agreement with ContentHeader.less and fidelity.spec.ts

	Core owns the h1 markup, so the hero type cannot hang off a Blitzy class on the heading
	element itself. The assertion selectors are therefore:

	  .blitzy-content-header .mw-first-heading   88px at 1440px, 56px at 768px, 40px at 390px
	  .blitzy-content-header__tagline            19px at 1440px and at 768px, 17px at 390px

	ContentHeader.less must reach the heading through that descendant selector, and resolve
	the values from tokens.less: @blitzy-font-size-display-wide, -mid and -narrow for the
	heading; @blitzy-font-size-subhead-wide and -narrow for the tagline;
	@blitzy-line-height-display (0.98), @blitzy-font-family-display and
	@blitzy-color-text-primary for the heading treatment -- switched on
	@blitzy-min-width-breakpoint-tablet (768px) and @blitzy-min-width-breakpoint-desktop
	(1024px). No length, colour or font is declared here: this file carries no style
	attribute and no colour literal (R3).

	768px is the trap width. The middle display band spans 768px to 1023px, so the heading is
	the only value in the entire skin that changes at exactly 768px -- the navigation card is
	still its full 76px because that disclosure applies below 768px, and the card grid is
	still two columns because that applies at and above 768px. Nothing below is conditionally
	omitted on viewport or on state, because a computed-style assertion against an absent
	element fails rather than skips (R11).

	Component 11's hook is .blitzy-tabs, nested as the last child of .blitzy-content-header
	so that this partial has a single root element and one predictable insertion point.

	The 2.5px active-tab underline resolves in Cards.less against the class core puts on the
	current item, and that class is `selected`, NOT `active`. Verified by rendering
	Ada Lovelace on MediaWiki 1.43.9: the current tab arrives as
	class="selected mw-list-item". SkinTemplate::tabAction() and the content-navigation
	builders set 'class' => 'selected' on the current tab, whereas the 'active' flag that
	SkinComponentListItem turns into an `active` class is used by the personal and user
	menus, not by these four. Styling only .active here would silently produce no underline
	at all, which is why the distinction is recorded rather than left to be discovered. Both
	are cheap to cover, so Cards.less should accept either.

	NOT EMITTED HERE, DELIBERATELY

	  A hand-rolled h1. Rebuilding the heading from html-title would leave the
	  aria-labelledby="firstHeading" reference on #bodyContent pointing at a second element,
	  put two h1 elements or two id="firstHeading" attributes on the page -- a blocking R10
	  failure on all twenty-one captures -- and lose core's blanked-heading and
	  user-language handling. Core's style="display: none" arrives inside the raw string and
	  is core's to emit; it is not stripped.

	  #contentSub, #contentSub2, html-subtitle and html-undelete-link. Those belong to
	  ContentBody.mustache and are emitted exactly once, there. The requirement that this
	  header sit over the tagline, indicator and subtitle regions is met by visual stacking,
	  not by duplicating elements; a second #contentSub would be a duplicate id and would
	  break the editor-facing contract core annotates on it.

	  The message `sitesubtitle`. It does not exist in core's message file, so referencing it
	  would fail the banana checker and render nothing.

	  html-user-language-attributes. It emits a dir attribute, and this file carries no
	  directional attribute and makes no markup-order assumption; tab alignment and the
	  current-tab underline are flip-aware stylesheet concerns (R6).

	  Anything beyond the title, the tagline, the indicators and the four tab groups. No
	  breadcrumb, no byline, no share control, no reading-time estimate (R12).

	ACCESSIBLE NAMES

	Four nav landmarks appear here and the page carries more elsewhere, so each one is named
	(R10). The names come from core messages rather than from each portlet's own `label`,
	because SkinComponentMenu::getMenuLabel() falls back to the raw menu name when no message
	exists: `associated-pages` has no core message, so `label` would render the literal
	string "associated-pages" as that landmark's accessible name -- clean to an automated
	scan, meaningless to a screen-reader user. The core message `namespaces` is used instead,
	which is also what this region has historically been called. The other three groups use
	msg-views, msg-actions and msg-variants for symmetry and so that skin.json's message list
	documents the whole set. Note that `label` would read "More" for the actions group, since
	getMenuLabel() runs after the rename described next.

	THE p-cactions RENAME

	SkinComponentMenu renames the `actions` menu to `cactions` before deriving its id, so the
	data member data-actions yields id="p-cactions" and class "mw-portlet mw-portlet-cactions".
	Core does this on purpose, for compatibility with gadgets that have targeted #p-cactions
	since MonoBook. Both values are re-emitted verbatim and neither is "corrected" to
	p-actions (R2, R7). Every portlet id and class is re-emitted as core supplied it, with the
	Blitzy class placed alongside rather than instead of it, and every item is emitted through
	html-item so that core's mw-list-item class, its `selected` class on the current tab, its
	per-item id, title and accesskey attributes and any extra class such as mw-watchlink all
	survive untouched. html-item is used rather than rebuilding the list item around the inner
	html, which is the alternative core allows but which would mean re-declaring every one of
	those attributes by hand.

	The space before html-tooltip is deliberate. Linker::tooltip() returns an attribute
	fragment produced by Xml::expandAttributes(), which prefixes every attribute with its own
	space, so the tag is well formed either way; keeping the space means the tag stays well
	formed even if that fragment ever arrives unprefixed. When a portlet has no tooltip the
	only consequence is one space before the closing angle bracket, which is valid and
	byte-stable.

	THE TWO-LAYER GUARD ON EACH GROUP

	The section on the dotted path suppresses a group whose menu core did not supply at all.
	It cannot suppress an empty menu, because a portlet is always a non-empty hash -- it has
	an id and a class even with no items -- so the inverted section on is-empty does that
	second job. Both layers are needed: on a monolingual wiki data-variants exists with
	is-empty true, and it must produce nothing at all rather than a named landmark wrapped
	around an empty list. html-before-portal and html-after-portal are passed through for the
	same reason they are counted in is-empty: they carry SkinAfterPortlet output, so dropping
	them would discard extension content from a menu that the guard had already decided was
	worth rendering.

	NOTES FOR ContentHeader.less

	The indicators wrapper carries core's mw-indicators class alongside the Blitzy one, so
	gadgets and extensions that append into .mw-indicators keep working and core's
	interface-indicators feature -- declared in skin.json and consisting of a single
	float rule on that class -- is not defeated by the re-wrap (R8). Because indicators are
	emitted after the heading here rather than before it, that inherited float has to be
	dealt with explicitly in the stylesheet rather than relied upon. The wrapper itself is
	unconditional, which keeps the DOM stable across pages with and without indicators and
	gives those gadgets a node to append to either way.

	Each group also carries a modifier class so the namespace tabs, the view tabs, the
	actions menu and the variants selector can be laid out differently without the stylesheet
	having to reach for core's mw-portlet- classes.

	One measured warning about the tab links, recorded because it is invisible in the markup.
	The tab cluster is the only closely spaced interactive group this partial produces, and
	with no stylesheet present its links render at the browser's default inline line box:
	17px tall with roughly 12px between neighbours. Both axe-core 4.13.0 and Lighthouse
	13.4.0 report that as a serious target-size finding on eight nodes, which R10 treats as
	blocking. Nothing in the markup causes it and no markup change can fix it -- the size and
	spacing are the stylesheet's to set, and core's @min-size-interactive-touch (44px, the
	same figure the design gives the navigation call-to-action pills) is the floor to reach
	for. If the design deliberately specifies a smaller target, implement the design and flag
	it for review; what must not happen is the defaults being left as they are.

	Both wrappers can legitimately render empty and still have to be there, because R11's
	assertions fail rather than skip against an absent element. Verified on the 1.43.9
	instance: Special:RecentChanges produces no tabs and no indicators, so both come out as
	empty elements, while an article produces three groups as an anonymous user and four only
	where language variants exist. Any border, underline or padding on .blitzy-tabs or on
	.mw-indicators therefore needs an :empty guard, or a stray rule will appear on the special
	pages in the capture matrix.
}}
<div class="blitzy-content-header">
	<div class="blitzy-content-header__title">{{{html-title-heading}}}</div>
	<p class="blitzy-content-header__tagline">{{msg-tagline}}</p>
	<div class="mw-indicators blitzy-content-header__indicators">
{{#array-indicators}}
		<div id="{{id}}" class="{{class}}">{{{html}}}</div>
{{/array-indicators}}
	</div>
	<div class="blitzy-tabs">
{{#data-portlets.data-associated-pages}}
{{^is-empty}}
		<nav class="blitzy-tabs__group blitzy-tabs__group--associated-pages" aria-label="{{msg-namespaces}}">
			<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-tabs__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}" {{{html-tooltip}}}>
				{{{html-before-portal}}}<ul class="blitzy-tabs__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{{html-after-portal}}}
			</div>
		</nav>
{{/is-empty}}
{{/data-portlets.data-associated-pages}}
{{#data-portlets.data-views}}
{{^is-empty}}
		<nav class="blitzy-tabs__group blitzy-tabs__group--views" aria-label="{{msg-views}}">
			<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-tabs__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}" {{{html-tooltip}}}>
				{{{html-before-portal}}}<ul class="blitzy-tabs__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{{html-after-portal}}}
			</div>
		</nav>
{{/is-empty}}
{{/data-portlets.data-views}}
{{#data-portlets.data-actions}}
{{^is-empty}}
		<nav class="blitzy-tabs__group blitzy-tabs__group--actions" aria-label="{{msg-actions}}">
			<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-tabs__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}" {{{html-tooltip}}}>
				{{{html-before-portal}}}<ul class="blitzy-tabs__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{{html-after-portal}}}
			</div>
		</nav>
{{/is-empty}}
{{/data-portlets.data-actions}}
{{#data-portlets.data-variants}}
{{^is-empty}}
		<nav class="blitzy-tabs__group blitzy-tabs__group--variants" aria-label="{{msg-variants}}">
			<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-tabs__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}" {{{html-tooltip}}}>
				{{{html-before-portal}}}<ul class="blitzy-tabs__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{{html-after-portal}}}
			</div>
		</nav>
{{/is-empty}}
{{/data-portlets.data-variants}}
	</div>
</div>
